## Considerations

- The amount of manual snapshots per AWS account is limited, so it's important to cleanup old snapshots
- Daily snapshots are provided "out of the box", and **do not count** towards the "Manual Snapshots" total
- Managing snapshots is the teams' responsibility (as are snapshot restores), so teams are responsible for cleaning up unneeded manual snapshots in order to avoid hitting our AWS account limits
- **Manual snapshots persist** even after the source database is deleted. Automated snapshots are deleted when the source DB is deleted
- Manual snapshot creation takes approximately **2-3 minutes**
- Before starting, check that you don't have [skip_final_snapshot](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/cloud-platform-terraform-rds-instance?tab=readme-ov-file#input_skip_final_snapshot) set to true, unless you are really happy to proceed without that backup.
- You cannot restore a snapshot taken from an older DB engine version to a newer DB engine version, or vice versa. The engine version of the snapshot must match the engine version of the target DB instance when restoring from a snapshot.

## Restoring live services from an RDS DB snapshot

> **Note:** If your existing RDS instance has deletion protection enabled, you must first disable it before performing a restore.
>
> **Important:** Database read-write operations are not availiable during the database restore step. This can take some time. Carry out testing on a representative test database to see how long your restore will take.


There are two restore scenarios:

- **Scenario A:** Restore to the SAME database (replace existing)
- **Scenario B:** Restore to a NEW database (cross-DB restore)

---

### Before you start (both scenarios)

Complete these checks before raising any PR. They apply to both Scenario A and Scenario B.

#### Check the allocated storage

Restoring fails if the target database has less storage than the database the snapshot came from. The apply ends with:

```
InvalidParameterCombination: Invalid storage size for engine name postgres and storage type gp3: <size>
```

Check the storage recorded against the snapshot you intend to use:

```bash
aws rds describe-db-snapshots \
  --db-snapshot-identifier <snapshot-name> \
  --query 'DBSnapshots[0].[DBSnapshotIdentifier,AllocatedStorage,EngineVersion]' \
  --output text \
  --region eu-west-2
```

Compare that `AllocatedStorage` figure against the `db_allocated_storage` value in your RDS module. If you have not set it, the module default is 20GB. `db_allocated_storage` must be at least the snapshot's `AllocatedStorage`.

If you need to raise it, AWS requires any increase in allocated storage to be at least 10% of the current value, so a smaller change is rejected. See [Increasing DB instance storage capacity](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_PIOPS.ModifyingExisting.html).

> **Warning:** You cannot use a snapshot restore to reduce storage. The size shown against a snapshot in the AWS console reflects the data in use rather than the volume provisioned, so it is not the figure to size from. Use `AllocatedStorage` as above. To shrink a database, dump the data logically and load it into a new, smaller instance, as described in [Migrating an RDS instance](https://user-guide.cloud-platform.service.justice.gov.uk/documentation/other-topics/aws-rds-migration.html).

#### Use a manual snapshot

Restore from a manual snapshot. Automated snapshots (those prefixed `rds:`) are tied to the lifecycle of the source instance and are deleted when it is deleted. Terraform destroys the existing instance before creating its replacement, so an automated snapshot can disappear part-way through a restore.

> **Important:** Before you start either scenario, take a manual snapshot of the database as it stands now. Restoring deletes the current database along with its automated backups, so without one there is no way back to the state the database is in today. Use the `create-db-snapshot` command below.

List what already exists for the database. The `SnapshotType` column tells you whether each one is manual or automated:

```bash
aws rds describe-db-snapshots \
  --db-instance-identifier <rds_name> \
  --query 'DBSnapshots[*].[DBSnapshotIdentifier,SnapshotCreateTime,SnapshotType,Status]' \
  --output table \
  --region eu-west-2
```

You then need a manual snapshot to restore from. Depending on which point in time you need, either create a new one or copy an existing automated one.

Create a manual snapshot to capture the database as it stands now. The suggested name format is `rds-name-namespace-purpose-date`:

```bash
aws rds create-db-snapshot \
  --db-instance-identifier <rds_name> \
  --db-snapshot-identifier <snapshot-name> \
  --region eu-west-2
```

Copy an automated snapshot to a manual one where the point in time you need is only held in an automated snapshot:

```bash
aws rds copy-db-snapshot \
  --source-db-snapshot-identifier <automated-snapshot-id> \
  --target-db-snapshot-identifier <new-manual-snapshot-name> \
  --copy-tags \
  --region eu-west-2
```

#### Confirm the snapshot belongs to the right database

Check the snapshot's source instance, engine version and master username before using it. `db_engine_version` and `rds_family` in your config must match the snapshot.

```bash
aws rds describe-db-snapshots \
  --db-snapshot-identifier <snapshot-name> \
  --query 'DBSnapshots[0].[DBInstanceIdentifier,InstanceCreateTime,EngineVersion,AllocatedStorage,MasterUsername]' \
  --output text
```

Restoring a snapshot that belongs to a different database carries that database's credentials and encryption key across with it. See [The restored database has the wrong username or password](#the-restored-database-has-the-wrong-username-or-password).

#### Check for a final snapshot name clash

When Terraform destroys a database it writes a final snapshot named `<rds_name>-finalsnapshot`. That name is fixed, so a snapshot left over from an earlier restore will block the apply. Check whether one already exists:

```bash
aws rds describe-db-snapshots \
  --db-snapshot-identifier <rds_name>-finalsnapshot \
  --region eu-west-2
```

If it does, see [`DBSnapshotAlreadyExists`](#dbsnapshotalreadyexists).

#### Check for a read replica

If the database has a read replica, it must be dealt with before restoring. See [Read replicas](#read-replicas).

