## Troubleshooting

### DBSnapshotAlreadyExists

```
Error: deleting RDS DB Instance (<rds_name>): DBSnapshotAlreadyExists: Cannot create
the snapshot because a snapshot with the identifier <rds_name>-finalsnapshot already exists.
```

Terraform destroys the existing instance before creating its replacement, and on the way out it writes a final snapshot named `<rds_name>-finalsnapshot`. That name is fixed, so a snapshot left over from an earlier restore blocks the apply.

Confirm the data you need is held elsewhere, then delete the conflicting snapshot and re-run the build:

```bash
aws rds delete-db-snapshot \
  --db-snapshot-identifier <rds_name>-finalsnapshot \
  --region eu-west-2
```

> **Warning:** Check what that snapshot contains before deleting it. If your database has already been replaced once, `<rds_name>-finalsnapshot` may hold the only copy of the data from before that happened. Compare its `InstanceCreateTime` against the database you expect it to have come from, and copy it to a safely named manual snapshot if you are unsure.

### Setting skip_final_snapshot does not avoid the clash

Adding `skip_final_snapshot = true` in the same PR as `snapshot_identifier` will not prevent the error above.

Changing `snapshot_identifier` forces Terraform to replace the instance, and the destroy half of a replacement uses the values already recorded in state, not the values in your new PR. `skip_final_snapshot = true` only takes effect once it has been applied and stored in state, which cannot happen in the same run.

Delete the conflicting snapshot instead.

### The restored database has the wrong username or password

The master username and password are generated by the Terraform module and held in state, but a restored instance keeps the master username from the snapshot.

If you restore from a snapshot belonging to a different database, such as another environment or namespace, the restored instance comes up with that database's credentials, which will not match what your application expects. Your pods will fail to connect even though the restore itself reported success.

A snapshot from another database also carries that database's KMS encryption key, and the restored instance inherits that key rather than using your own. If you need to restore from a snapshot belonging to a different database, raise it in [#ask-cloud-platform](https://mojdt.slack.com/archives/C57UPMZLY) before you proceed.

Always check the snapshot's source instance before using it. See [Confirm the snapshot belongs to the right database](#confirm-the-snapshot-belongs-to-the-right-database).

### Read replicas

If the database has a read replica, Terraform fails with:

```
Error: cannot elect new source database for replication
```

When a source database is destroyed, AWS promotes its read replica to a standalone instance, and a promoted instance can no longer be used as a replication target. Terraform therefore has no way to reattach it to the restored database, and re-running the build will not clear this. See [Considerations when deleting replicas](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/UserGuide/USER_ReadRepl.html#USER_ReadRepl.Overview.deletion-considerations).

The replica has to be destroyed and rebuilt, in two separate PRs:

1. Set `count = 0` on the read replica module and on the `kubernetes_secret` resource for the replica. Both must change together, or the plan fails with `module.read_replica is empty tuple`. Merge and let it apply.
2. Set both back to `count = 1`. This builds a fresh replica from the restored database.

The rebuilt replica has a new database identifier and endpoint, so restart any pods that read from it.

If you want the promoted replica's data kept, snapshot it before step 1. The module sets `skip_final_snapshot = true` and `db_backup_retention_period = 0` on replicas, so nothing is retained when one is deleted.

### Permission denied copying a snapshot

Copying an encrypted snapshot from the service pod may fail with an access denied error. Raise this in [#ask-cloud-platform](https://mojdt.slack.com/archives/C57UPMZLY) with the error message, and the Cloud Platform team can carry out the copy for you.
